// Practice quiz: Adding the FastAPI service and its database to Compose
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/adding-fastapi-service-to-compose

::o1 build key::A Compose service is made from a Dockerfile in the folder ./worker. Which setting says so? {
=build\: ./worker#Correct. build points Compose at the folder with the Dockerfile.
~image\: ./worker#Not quite. image names an image to pull, not a build folder.
~volumes\: ./worker#Not quite. That would mount files, not build anything.
~context\: ./worker at the top of the file#Not quite. context belongs inside a build section.
}

::o1 own volume::Two PostgreSQL services run in one Compose file. How should their data be stored? {
~Both mount the same named volume#Not quite. Two servers must not share one data directory.
=Each mounts its own named volume#Correct. Separate volumes keep their data files apart.
~Neither needs a volume, because Compose saves containers#Not quite. Without a volume the data goes when the container is removed.
~Both write to the image#Not quite. Images are read-only templates.
}

::o2 url host::A FastAPI service uses a Compose database service called search-db. Which DATABASE_URL host is right? {
~localhost#Not quite. That is the FastAPI container itself.
~127.0.0.1#Not quite. That is also the FastAPI container itself.
=search-db#Correct. Service names resolve on the Compose network.
~the laptop hostname#Not quite. Containers on one Compose network talk by service name.
}

::o2 password::How should a compose.yaml supply a disposable local database password? {
~As a literal value in compose.yaml#Not quite. Tracked files must not hold the value.
~In the Dockerfile with ENV#Not quite. That bakes it into the image.
~In the service name#Not quite. Names are visible everywhere.
=Through a reference such as $\{PASSWORD\}, with its value in an untracked env file#Correct. It keeps the value out of the tracked file.
}

::o3 why::Why can a server bound to 127.0.0.1 in a container not be reached from another container? {
=Traffic from elsewhere arrives on a different interface, where the server is not listening#Correct. Loopback only accepts connections from inside that container.
~Containers block all incoming traffic#Not quite. They accept traffic on interfaces a server listens on.
~127.0.0.1 is reserved for databases#Not quite. It is not reserved for any kind of program.
~Compose rewrites the port#Not quite. Compose does not rewrite the address the server listens on.
}

::o3 ports confusion::A file has ports\: "127.0.0.1\:9000\:9000" and the server listens on 0.0.0.0. What does 127.0.0.1 in that ports line control? {
~Which address the server listens on in the container#Not quite. That is set by the server command.
=Which address on the host publishes the port#Correct. It limits the host side and leaves the bind address in the container alone.
~Which DNS name other services use#Not quite. Service names handle that.
~Whether the port uses HTTPS#Not quite. It has nothing to do with TLS.
}
